#
# A model recommendation asserted AS PROOF — the third authority position, and the one
# an advisory reaches without ever touching a pin.
#
# logic:Advisory's own definition bars it from three positions: authorizing a pin,
# deciding an approval, and carrying a proof. The pin position has a shipped negative
# (advisory-authorizes-a-pin.ttl). This is the proof position, which is the more
# dangerous of the two precisely because nothing about the record LOOKS like an
# authorization: the model output is simply typed logic:AuthorizationProof alongside
# logic:Advisory and pointed at the intent whose licensing it claims to establish.
# Downstream, "the intent has a proof" is true, checkable, and worthless — the proof
# term is a recommendation, and a checker asked to recompute it can only re-run the
# model and get another recommendation.
#
# What it costs in the world: the payment dispatches. Every subsequent audit finds an
# authorization proof bound to the intent and stops looking, because that is what an
# authorization proof is for.
#
# MUST trip logic:AdvisoryNeverAuthorityConstraint.

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix ex:    <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/logic/tests/> .

ex:modelSaysTheChargeIsInPolicy a logic:Advisory , logic:AuthorizationProof ;
    rdfs:label "a model recommendation retyped as the proof that licensed the charge"@en ;
    logic:advisoryAbout ex:payVendorStep ;
    logic:proofEstablishes ex:payVendorIntent .
